  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense never arrived. One hit and ten strikeouts against Seattle is not a competitive game. It's an abdication. Gage Jump gave them four innings of two-run ball, the bullpen held everything intact, and none of it mattered because the lineup simply did not show up. This is how losses stack when pitching does its job and nothing else answers.",
    "what_this_means": "A team that leaves runners stranded in the fourth and never generates another threat is a team that has stopped competing. Jump exited after four, but Seth Johnson and Scott Blewett combined for three scoreless innings with five strikeouts. The pitching gave them every chance to stay in it. The bats made that irrelevant. Strikeout totals like this are not variance. They are a statement about how completely this offense checked out. One hit through nine innings is not a slump. It's a warning that inconsistency has teeth, and when it shows up, it erases everything else. They needed clean innings and got them. They needed runs and got nothing. The math there is simple.",
    "what_to_watch": "Dylan Cease takes the mound tonight for Toronto with a 2.25 ERA. Jacob Lopez has pitched to a 2.35 mark over his last four starts and will need every bit of that sharpness. This is not a matchup where offensive help is coming easy.",
